WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.714 --> 00:00:15.532
Here's a question we should all be asking:

00:00:15.556 --> 00:00:17.325
What went wrong?

00:00:17.349 --> 00:00:19.207
Not just with the pandemic

00:00:19.231 --> 00:00:21.231
but with our civic life.

00:00:21.544 --> 00:00:26.226
What brought us to this polarized,
rancorous political moment?

00:00:27.147 --> 00:00:28.601
In recent decades,

00:00:28.625 --> 00:00:32.895
the divide between winners and losers
has been deepening,

00:00:32.919 --> 00:00:34.910
poisoning our politics,

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:37.133
setting us apart.

00:00:37.157 --> 00:00:41.042
This divide is partly about inequality.

00:00:41.577 --> 00:00:46.224
But it's also about the attitudes
toward winning and losing

00:00:46.248 --> 00:00:47.835
that have come with it.

00:00:47.859 --> 00:00:49.510
Those who landed on top

00:00:49.534 --> 00:00:53.791
came to believe that their success
was their own doing,

00:00:54.196 --> 00:00:56.196
a measure of their merit,

00:00:56.522 --> 00:01:00.950
and that those who lost out
had no one to blame but themselves.

00:01:02.177 --> 00:01:04.558
This way of thinking about success

00:01:04.582 --> 00:01:08.000
arises from a seemingly
attractive principle.

00:01:08.772 --> 00:01:11.180
If everyone has an equal chance,

00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:14.180
the winners deserve their winnings.

00:01:14.927 --> 00:01:18.743
This is the heart
of the meritocratic ideal.

00:01:19.849 --> 00:01:23.331
In practice, of course, we fall far short.

00:01:24.581 --> 00:01:27.986
Not everybody has an equal chance to rise.

00:01:29.006 --> 00:01:33.918
Children born to poor families
tend to stay poor when they grow up.

00:01:34.648 --> 00:01:39.535
Affluent parents are able to pass
their advantages onto their kids.

00:01:40.083 --> 00:01:43.607
At Ivy League universities, for example,

00:01:43.631 --> 00:01:47.031
there are more students
from the top one percent

00:01:47.055 --> 00:01:51.936
than from the entire bottom half
of the country combined.

00:01:53.627 --> 00:01:57.647
But the problem isn't only
that we fail to live up

00:01:57.671 --> 00:02:00.583
to the meritocratic
principles we proclaim.

00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:04.458
The ideal itself is flawed.

00:02:04.792 --> 00:02:06.426
It has a dark side.

00:02:06.887 --> 00:02:10.958
Meritocracy is corrosive
of the common good.

00:02:11.839 --> 00:02:14.344
It leads to hubris among the winners

00:02:15.538 --> 00:02:19.208
and humiliation among those who lose out.

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:25.643
It encourages the successful
to inhale too deeply of their success,

00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:30.542
to forget the luck and good fortune
that helped them on their way.

00:02:31.002 --> 00:02:35.383
And it leads them to look down
on those less fortunate,

00:02:35.407 --> 00:02:38.176
less credentialed than themselves.

00:02:39.085 --> 00:02:41.569
This matters for politics.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:47.450
One of the most potent sources
of the populous backlash

00:02:47.474 --> 00:02:52.807
is the sense among many working people
that elites look down on them.

00:02:53.708 --> 00:02:55.708
It's a legitimate complaint.

00:02:56.589 --> 00:03:01.855
Even as globalization
brought deepening inequality

00:03:01.879 --> 00:03:03.284
and stagnant wages,

00:03:04.506 --> 00:03:09.279
its proponents offered workers
some bracing advice.

00:03:10.359 --> 00:03:13.819
"If you want to compete and win
in the global economy,

00:03:13.843 --> 00:03:15.311
go to college."

00:03:15.776 --> 00:03:18.750
"What you earn depends on what you learn."

00:03:19.125 --> 00:03:21.125
"You can make it if you try."

00:03:22.327 --> 00:03:28.216
These elites miss the insult
implicit in this advice.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:31.125
If you don't go to college,

00:03:31.149 --> 00:03:34.645
if you don't flourish in the new economy,

00:03:34.669 --> 00:03:37.292
your failure is your fault.

00:03:37.819 --> 00:03:39.493
That's the implication.

00:03:39.851 --> 00:03:45.500
It's no wonder many working people
turned against meritocratic elites.

00:03:46.506 --> 00:03:48.220
So what should we do?

00:03:48.609 --> 00:03:52.664
We need to rethink three aspects
of our civic life.

00:03:53.331 --> 00:03:54.641
The role of college,

00:03:54.665 --> 00:03:56.244
the dignity of work

00:03:56.268 --> 00:03:57.982
and the meaning of success.

00:03:59.149 --> 00:04:03.297
We should begin by rethinking
the role of universities

00:04:03.321 --> 00:04:06.000
as arbiters of opportunity.

00:04:07.583 --> 00:04:12.460
For those of us who spend our days
in the company of the credentialed,

00:04:12.484 --> 00:04:15.753
it's easy to forget a simple fact:

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:20.525
Most people don't have
a four-year college degree.

00:04:21.077 --> 00:04:25.208
In fact, nearly two-thirds
of Americans don't.

00:04:25.978 --> 00:04:30.042
So it is folly to create an economy

00:04:30.066 --> 00:04:35.243
that makes a university diploma
a necessary condition

00:04:35.267 --> 00:04:38.542
of dignified work and a decent life.

00:04:39.307 --> 00:04:42.958
Encouraging people to go
to college is a good thing.

00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:45.760
Broadening access
for those who can't afford it

00:04:45.784 --> 00:04:47.148
is even better.

00:04:47.553 --> 00:04:50.125
But this is not a solution to inequality.

00:04:50.948 --> 00:04:56.424
We should focus less on arming people
for meritocratic combat,

00:04:56.448 --> 00:05:00.182
and focus more on making life better

00:05:00.206 --> 00:05:02.619
for people who lack a diploma

00:05:02.643 --> 00:05:06.777
but who make essential
contributions to our society.

00:05:07.728 --> 00:05:10.172
We should renew the dignity of work

00:05:10.196 --> 00:05:12.751
and place it at the center
of our politics.

00:05:13.272 --> 00:05:18.295
We should remember that work
is not only about making a living,

00:05:18.319 --> 00:05:21.744
it's also about contributing
to the common good

00:05:21.768 --> 00:05:24.542
and winning recognition for doing so.

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:28.672
Robert F. Kennedy put it well
half a century ago.

00:05:29.117 --> 00:05:32.800
Fellowship, community, shared patriotism.

00:05:33.438 --> 00:05:36.772
These essential values do not come

00:05:36.796 --> 00:05:39.667
from just buying and consuming
goods together.

00:05:40.863 --> 00:05:43.347
They come from dignified employment,

00:05:43.371 --> 00:05:45.156
at decent pay.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:48.938
The kind of employment
that enables us to say,

00:05:48.962 --> 00:05:50.962
"I helped to build this country.

00:05:51.522 --> 00:05:55.500
I am a participant
in its great public ventures."

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.522
This civic sentiment

00:05:59.546 --> 00:06:03.625
is largely missing
from our public life today.

00:06:04.788 --> 00:06:08.565
We often assume that the money people make

00:06:08.589 --> 00:06:11.878
is the measure of their contribution
to the common good.

00:06:12.807 --> 00:06:14.624
But this is a mistake.

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:18.167
Martin Luther King Jr. explained why.

00:06:19.355 --> 00:06:23.117
Reflecting on a strike
by sanitation workers

00:06:23.141 --> 00:06:25.142
in Memphis, Tennessee,

00:06:25.166 --> 00:06:27.166
shortly before he was assassinated,

00:06:28.097 --> 00:06:29.684
King said,

00:06:29.708 --> 00:06:34.732
"The person who picks up our garbage
is, in the final analysis,

00:06:34.756 --> 00:06:37.438
as significant as the physician,

00:06:38.855 --> 00:06:41.085
for if he doesn't do his job,

00:06:41.109 --> 00:06:43.109
diseases are rampant.

00:06:44.014 --> 00:06:46.792
All labor has dignity."

00:06:47.637 --> 00:06:50.065
Today's pandemic makes this clear.

00:06:50.589 --> 00:06:53.633
It reveals how deeply we rely

00:06:53.657 --> 00:06:56.664
on workers we often overlook.

00:06:57.567 --> 00:06:59.188
Delivery workers,

00:06:59.212 --> 00:07:01.093
maintenance workers,

00:07:01.117 --> 00:07:03.014
grocery store clerks,

00:07:03.038 --> 00:07:04.709
warehouse workers,

00:07:04.733 --> 00:07:06.093
truckers,

00:07:06.117 --> 00:07:07.744
nurse assistants,

00:07:07.768 --> 00:07:09.410
childcare workers,

00:07:09.434 --> 00:07:11.434
home health care providers.

00:07:11.776 --> 00:07:16.207
These are not the best-paid
or most honored workers.

00:07:17.234 --> 00:07:21.170
But now, we see them as essential workers.

00:07:22.337 --> 00:07:26.142
This is a moment for a public debate

00:07:26.166 --> 00:07:29.791
about how to bring their pay
and recognition

00:07:29.815 --> 00:07:33.926
into better alignment
with the importance of their work.

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:40.649
It is also time for a moral,
even spiritual, turning,

00:07:40.673 --> 00:07:44.374
questioning our meritocratic hubris.

00:07:45.645 --> 00:07:49.898
Do I morally deserve the talents
that enable me to flourish?

00:07:50.533 --> 00:07:52.467
Is it my doing

00:07:52.491 --> 00:07:56.133
that I live in a society
that prizes the talents

00:07:56.157 --> 00:07:57.875
I happen to have?

00:07:58.315 --> 00:08:00.315
Or is that my good luck?

00:08:01.042 --> 00:08:04.883
Insisting that my success is my due

00:08:04.907 --> 00:08:09.255
makes it hard to see myself
in other people's shoes.

00:08:10.188 --> 00:08:13.029
Appreciating the role of luck in life

00:08:13.053 --> 00:08:15.053
can prompt a certain humility.

00:08:15.879 --> 00:08:20.196
There but for the accident of birth,
or the grace of God,

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:22.094
or the mystery of fate,

00:08:22.118 --> 00:08:23.375
go I.

00:08:24.379 --> 00:08:26.736
This spirit of humility

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:29.355
is the civic virtue we need now.

00:08:30.101 --> 00:08:32.522
It's the beginning of a way back

00:08:32.546 --> 00:08:36.792
from the harsh ethic of success
that drives us apart.

00:08:37.466 --> 00:08:41.572
It points us beyond the tyranny of merit

00:08:41.596 --> 00:08:45.714
to a less rancorous,
more generous public life.

